WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.210
- [Kim] Hey this is Kim from Khan Academy,

00:00:02.210 --> 00:00:06.040
and today I'm talking with some experts

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:09.040
about the Fourth Amendment.

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:11.710
This is the Fourth Amendment
of the Bill of Rights,

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:13.730
and the Fourth Amendment deals with

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:17.150
unreasonable search and seizure.

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:19.280
So here's the official
text of the amendment.

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:22.340
It says, "The right of
the people to be secure

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:26.190
in their persons, houses,
papers, and effects,

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:28.820
against unreasonable
searches and seizures,

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:32.380
shall not be violated, and
no Warrants shall issue,

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:37.150
but upon probably cause,
supported by Oath or affirmation,

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:40.040
and particularly describing
the place to be searched,

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:42.810
and the persons or things to be seized."

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:44.550
To learn more about the Fourth Amendment,

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:46.950
I talked to two law professors

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:49.980
who specialize in criminal law,

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:53.240
Professor Tracey Meares of Yale University

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:56.930
and Professor Orin Kerr of
George Washington University,

00:00:56.930 --> 00:00:58.430
and he's a particular expert

00:00:58.430 --> 00:01:01.300
in criminal procedure and computer crime.

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:03.970
Professor Kerr, can you
tell us a little bit about

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:06.570
what the Fourth Amendment
is and why the Framers

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:10.030
were interested in protecting
these rights in particular?

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:11.720
- [Orin] The Fourth Amendment is a ban

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:14.840
on unreasonable searches and
seizures by the government.

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:18.030
It's something the Framers
were interested in because

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:20.370
they wanted to limit
the government's power.

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:21.770
At the time of the framing,

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:25.740
the English king had engaged
in some pretty bad things

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:27.100
that the Framers wanted to stop,

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:29.990
and one of those was
raiding people's homes

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:32.108
with what was called a general warrant.

00:01:32.108 --> 00:01:33.340
A general warrant was basically a warrant

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:35.370
that said the police could go anywhere

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:37.240
and look for anything without any limit.

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:38.910
- [Kim] I asked Professor
Meares to explain

00:01:38.910 --> 00:01:41.340
a little bit more about general warrants.

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:43.530
She's a professor at Yale Law School

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:45.720
who specializes in criminal procedure

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:47.530
and criminal law policy.

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:50.700
- [Tracey] They were a
general warrant allowing

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:55.700
these officials to search
people's homes, places, papers,

00:01:57.710 --> 00:02:00.910
the kinds of things that
the Fourth Amendment lists

00:02:01.985 --> 00:02:06.400
in ways that people thought was not okay.

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:09.530
The Fourth Amendment
was primarily concerned

00:02:09.530 --> 00:02:11.770
with a couple of things.

00:02:11.770 --> 00:02:16.770
One, a case involving
seditious libel investigation,

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:20.940
it's a case called Entick,

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:25.090
and a warrant was issued
authorizing Entick,

00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:26.440
that's the subject of the warrant,

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:31.263
his arrest and the seizure of
all of his books and papers

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:35.713
so that he could be prosecuted
for seditious libel.

00:02:36.713 --> 00:02:39.710
That case isn't as well
known as the second case,

00:02:39.710 --> 00:02:42.530
which is called the
Writs of Assistance case,

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:47.530
and basically the Writs
of Assistance involved

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:52.870
customs inspectors who
were trying to crack down

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:57.870
on smuggling that was
affecting Boston's economy.

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:04.680
So to aid in that task, inspectors
used writs of assistance

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:07.810
that were issued by the king

00:03:08.690 --> 00:03:13.690
and authorized the inspectors
both to draft assistance,

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:15.970
hence the name, writs of assistance,

00:03:15.970 --> 00:03:18.310
and to search any place where

00:03:18.310 --> 00:03:20.750
smuggled goods might be concealed.

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:22.440
- [Orin] The Framers wanted
to put an end to that,

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:24.700
and so they said no general
warrants are allowed

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:26.920
and searches and seizures
have to be reasonable.

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:29.600
That really means is the
government can't just stop anyone,

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:31.510
arrest anyone, break into someone's house.

00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:34.720
There are limits as to
what the government can do.

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:36.790
- [Kim] But what are those limits?

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:39.770
What determines probable
cause for a search,

00:03:39.770 --> 00:03:43.370
and what renders a search
and seizure unreasonable?

00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:45.030
- [Orin] Yeah, what counts as unreasonable

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:46.970
is a really big question.

00:03:46.970 --> 00:03:49.890
There are different answers
different courts have suggested,

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:52.480
but the basic idea is that first,

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:54.230
if the government has a valid warrant,

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:55.870
a search or seizure is reasonable,

00:03:55.870 --> 00:03:58.647
if the warrant, if a judge
has signed off and said,

00:03:58.647 --> 00:04:00.360
"Yeah the government has probable cause

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:02.380
to search this place for
particular evidence."

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:03.950
Then the government can do that.

00:04:03.950 --> 00:04:05.810
And then the Supreme
Court has laid out a bunch

00:04:05.810 --> 00:04:08.210
of what they call exceptions
to the warrant requirement,

00:04:08.210 --> 00:04:09.930
which are also reasonable searches.

00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:13.050
So for example, if a person
consents to a search,

00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:14.620
that's reasonable.

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:17.400
Or in the case of a car,
a car can be searched

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:19.440
without a warrant based on probable cause.

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:21.860
That's called the automobile exception.

00:04:21.860 --> 00:04:23.530
There are rules that
govern when the police

00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:25.410
can stop somebody temporarily.

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:27.380
When the government needs an arrest,

00:04:27.380 --> 00:04:28.610
they need probable cause.

00:04:28.610 --> 00:04:30.660
There are basically
all sorts of rules that

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:33.160
the Supreme Court has laid out
on a bunch of different cases

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:35.960
that say what counts as an
unreasonable search or seizure.

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:38.110
The Supreme Court has
said that probable cause

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:41.120
means a fair probability
that evidence will be found

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:42.760
in a particular place or

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:44.700
that a particular person
committed a crime.

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:47.570
So fair probability is the language.

00:04:47.570 --> 00:04:49.770
Now that's not something
the Supreme Court has said,

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:51.540
that's not 50%, that doesn't mean

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:52.980
it's more probable than not.

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:55.920
It just means there's
pretty good reason to think

00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:58.030
that there's evidence
in a particular place

00:04:58.030 --> 00:05:00.610
or a particular person committed a crime.

00:05:00.610 --> 00:05:01.800
So it doesn't mean a hunch.

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:03.294
It doesn't mean the officer just

00:05:03.294 --> 00:05:05.350
had the idea pop into his head.

00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:08.311
But it also doesn't mean
that the officer is certain.

00:05:08.311 --> 00:05:10.300
There's just gotta be
a pretty good reason,

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:11.850
but not certainty to think that

00:05:11.850 --> 00:05:14.670
that person committed the crime
but there's evidence there.

00:05:14.670 --> 00:05:16.750
- [Kim] But when I asked Professor
Meares the same question,

00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:19.630
she wasn't convinced
it was so easy to tell

00:05:19.630 --> 00:05:22.430
what counts as probable cause.

00:05:22.430 --> 00:05:27.430
So what counts as a true probable cause

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:30.089
to issue a warrant upon?

00:05:30.089 --> 00:05:32.050
- [Tracey] (sighs)

00:05:32.050 --> 00:05:34.940
- [Kim] Sorry, we could
just skip this instead.

00:05:34.940 --> 00:05:36.320
- [Tracey] Let's take a second.

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:37.823
I taught this today.

00:05:39.370 --> 00:05:41.710
These aren't questions that
you can just simply answer.

00:05:41.710 --> 00:05:44.320
No one can, actually.

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:48.470
There is, literally I taught today

00:05:48.470 --> 00:05:52.400
the fact that there is no real law

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:54.360
on what probable cause is.

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:57.570
It's inherently a factual determination.

00:05:57.570 --> 00:05:59.720
- [Kim] So how does this work in practice?

00:05:59.720 --> 00:06:01.810
I asked Professor Kerr
if it's the case that

00:06:01.810 --> 00:06:04.460
a police officer has to convince a judge

00:06:04.460 --> 00:06:06.970
that there's probable
cause to issue a warrant.

00:06:06.970 --> 00:06:08.830
- [Orin] Yeah, so it
can come up in two ways.

00:06:08.830 --> 00:06:10.850
In some cases, the Supreme Court says

00:06:10.850 --> 00:06:13.230
that the police have
to go to a judge first

00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:16.660
and get a warrant and then
they have to convince the judge

00:06:16.660 --> 00:06:19.700
that what they're about to do
is based on probable cause.

00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:21.850
In another case, it's come
up when the government

00:06:21.850 --> 00:06:24.300
has searched first or arrested first,

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:26.170
and then they come back with the evidence

00:06:26.170 --> 00:06:28.070
or come back with the
person who was arrested

00:06:28.070 --> 00:06:29.470
and then there's a hearing as to whether

00:06:29.470 --> 00:06:30.510
there was probable cause

00:06:30.510 --> 00:06:31.910
for the arrest that already occurred.

00:06:31.910 --> 00:06:33.640
So either way, the standards are the same.

00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:35.600
It's that fair probability idea.

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:37.080
Ultimately, up to a judge

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:38.660
whether the police were right or wrong

00:06:38.660 --> 00:06:40.270
in doing what they did.

00:06:40.270 --> 00:06:42.490
- [Kim] Interesting, so
there is a little bit of

00:06:42.490 --> 00:06:46.420
prior legwork involved in some cases.

00:06:46.420 --> 00:06:50.243
What happens in cases when
it might be really important

00:06:50.243 --> 00:06:55.243
to get evidence, say if
there's a bomb threat?

00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:57.465
- [Orin] So there are some rules

00:06:57.465 --> 00:07:01.050
that allow searches or
seizures without a warrant,

00:07:01.050 --> 00:07:03.070
under what they call
exigent circumstances.

00:07:03.070 --> 00:07:05.510
That basically means
there was an emergency.

00:07:05.510 --> 00:07:08.300
So the government had to act
without a search warrant.

00:07:08.300 --> 00:07:10.430
And so, if there's a ticking time bomb

00:07:10.430 --> 00:07:13.120
that they think is gonna
go off in a package

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:14.630
and they've got good
reason to think there is

00:07:14.630 --> 00:07:16.010
this ticking time bomb in the package,

00:07:16.010 --> 00:07:18.540
they don't have to say, hey
we're gonna go find a judge

00:07:18.540 --> 00:07:20.140
because by the time they do that,

00:07:20.140 --> 00:07:21.260
the bomb might have gone off.

00:07:21.260 --> 00:07:22.778
They can search that package

00:07:22.778 --> 00:07:25.393
under this exigent circumstances idea.

00:07:26.610 --> 00:07:28.950
So that's an exception to the rules,

00:07:28.950 --> 00:07:31.590
although beyond that,
there's no difference between

00:07:31.590 --> 00:07:34.030
a serious case and a less serious case

00:07:34.030 --> 00:07:34.863
under the Fourth Amendment.

00:07:34.863 --> 00:07:36.010
They're kind of all serious cases.

00:07:36.010 --> 00:07:39.220
So the courts have to follow
and the police have to follow

00:07:39.220 --> 00:07:42.650
the same rules in less serious
and more serious cases.

00:07:42.650 --> 00:07:44.270
- [Kim] Professor Meares
gave me an example

00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:46.660
of what seemed like a less serious case,

00:07:46.660 --> 00:07:49.010
but it's still a really
interesting example

00:07:49.010 --> 00:07:52.580
of what counts as an
unreasonable search and seizure.

00:07:52.580 --> 00:07:54.000
- [Tracey] I'll give you one more case

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:55.830
that I'm sure the high
school students would love

00:07:55.830 --> 00:07:58.320
and my students love it.

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:03.320
It's a case where a person
was sitting in a noodle shop

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:08.250
in front of a window, eating
a big bowl of noodles,

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:10.590
clear soup with noodles,

00:08:10.590 --> 00:08:14.510
and a police officer walks by
the person eating the noodles

00:08:14.510 --> 00:08:17.820
and notices something floating
in the bowl of noodles.

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:20.730
So he enters into the store

00:08:20.730 --> 00:08:22.540
where the person is eating the noodles.

00:08:22.540 --> 00:08:25.860
And the person has left
temporarily the bowl of noodles

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:28.683
to get a napkin or go to
the bathroom or something.

00:08:28.683 --> 00:08:31.970
And then the police officer
approaches the bowl,

00:08:31.970 --> 00:08:33.900
picks up the person's spoon,

00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:38.200
and stirs the noodles to
see if he can find anything,

00:08:38.200 --> 00:08:41.210
and some baggies of
crack float to the top.

00:08:41.210 --> 00:08:44.450
Did the person have a reasonable
expectation of privacy

00:08:44.450 --> 00:08:45.283
in their food?

00:08:45.283 --> 00:08:46.510
- [Kim] In their soup?

00:08:46.510 --> 00:08:47.760
- [Tracey] In their soup.

00:08:49.710 --> 00:08:51.200
- [Kim] How did they rule?

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:55.640
- [Tracey] They ruled that
the stirring was a search.

00:08:55.640 --> 00:09:00.440
So if it is a search, i.e.,
that you have a subject of

00:09:00.440 --> 00:09:02.850
interest in privacy in your soup

00:09:02.850 --> 00:09:06.300
that society is prepared
to recognize as reasonable,

00:09:06.300 --> 00:09:11.020
then the police officer
would have to get a warrant

00:09:11.020 --> 00:09:13.530
before stirring the soup.

00:09:13.530 --> 00:09:15.150
- [Kim] This raises the question,

00:09:15.150 --> 00:09:18.320
where do I have Fourth Amendment rights?

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:21.290
Are some locations
protected and not others?

00:09:21.290 --> 00:09:23.170
- [Orin] The building block
of the Fourth Amendment

00:09:23.170 --> 00:09:25.150
is really that you have
Fourth Amendment rights

00:09:25.150 --> 00:09:29.790
in your home, in your
pockets, in your packages,

00:09:29.790 --> 00:09:32.520
but you don't have Fourth
Amendment rights outside.

00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:34.800
You don't have Fourth
Amendment rights in what

00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:37.390
you're doing outside or
if the police want to walk

00:09:37.390 --> 00:09:40.260
on what they call open
fields, at public streets,

00:09:40.260 --> 00:09:43.050
you don't have Fourth
Amendment rights in that.

00:09:43.050 --> 00:09:45.150
So thinking about how the
Fourth Amendment applies

00:09:45.150 --> 00:09:46.240
just in the physical world,

00:09:46.240 --> 00:09:47.870
if the police want to
break into your home,

00:09:47.870 --> 00:09:48.990
they need a search warrant.

00:09:48.990 --> 00:09:51.810
If the police want to watch
you walk down the street,

00:09:51.810 --> 00:09:53.530
they don't need anything.

00:09:53.530 --> 00:09:55.120
It's not a search or a seizure,

00:09:55.120 --> 00:09:58.110
so they can do anything they
want, just watching you.

00:09:58.110 --> 00:09:59.510
If they want to arrest you,

00:09:59.510 --> 00:10:03.080
they need probable cause to
believe you committed a crime.

00:10:03.080 --> 00:10:05.120
So those are really the building blocks

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:07.260
of the Fourth Amendment that going back

00:10:07.260 --> 00:10:09.160
to 18th century technology.

00:10:09.160 --> 00:10:10.620
And then the question becomes, alright,

00:10:10.620 --> 00:10:13.860
well how do you update
that to a modern world?

00:10:13.860 --> 00:10:16.060
- [Tracey] It's also the case
that the Fourth Amendment

00:10:16.060 --> 00:10:18.770
applies to seizures,

00:10:18.770 --> 00:10:23.570
what police have to show
before they arrest a person

00:10:23.570 --> 00:10:26.650
or temporarily stop them

00:10:26.650 --> 00:10:31.360
or briefly pat their body on the outside,

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:33.030
their body for weapons.

00:10:33.030 --> 00:10:36.500
All of those questions are
Fourth Amendment questions

00:10:36.500 --> 00:10:40.680
as are questions involving how much force

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:44.830
police actually use to effect the arrest.

00:10:44.830 --> 00:10:48.790
Once we've decided that
an arrest is legitimate.

00:10:48.790 --> 00:10:51.380
So we haven't talked about
any of those questions,

00:10:51.380 --> 00:10:53.250
but the Fourth Amendment governs

00:10:53.250 --> 00:10:58.250
all of that and remember
the Fourth Amendment governs

00:10:58.460 --> 00:11:01.780
all of those topics in a context in which

00:11:01.780 --> 00:11:06.180
the Framers' motivations for
enacting the Fourth Amendment

00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:08.380
have absolutely nothing to do

00:11:08.380 --> 00:11:10.990
with most of those
things I just mentioned.

00:11:10.990 --> 00:11:13.330
- [Kim] But the Fourth
Amendment also has implications

00:11:13.330 --> 00:11:16.040
that go beyond warrants and seizures.

00:11:16.040 --> 00:11:18.120
Professor Meares thinks
that one of the biggest

00:11:18.120 --> 00:11:21.680
Fourth Amendment issues
today is discretion.

00:11:21.680 --> 00:11:24.210
- [Teresa] Many people have thought

00:11:24.210 --> 00:11:28.755
much more about how the Fourth
Amendment protects privacy

00:11:28.755 --> 00:11:33.130
than how the Fourth Amendment
addresses discretion.

00:11:33.130 --> 00:11:34.580
- [Kim] And by discretion in this case,

00:11:34.580 --> 00:11:37.640
you mean what they get to decide
to do on their own, right?

00:11:37.640 --> 00:11:39.430
- [Tracey] Exactly so.

00:11:39.430 --> 00:11:42.400
And one way to see the difference
between those two things

00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:47.400
is to think about a medal
detector in an airport.

00:11:48.200 --> 00:11:52.310
So long as everyone is
required to go through

00:11:52.310 --> 00:11:56.560
the metal protector, there
is no law enforcement

00:11:56.560 --> 00:11:59.040
discretion involved.

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:01.700
- [Kim] So this is kind
of where the public safety

00:12:01.700 --> 00:12:06.700
aspect comes in, that
if we're all subject to

00:12:08.500 --> 00:12:13.500
non-invasive procedures
at the whim of police.

00:12:14.310 --> 00:12:16.990
It's a little different
than if individuals

00:12:16.990 --> 00:12:19.720
are kind of plucked for that reason.

00:12:19.720 --> 00:12:23.300
- Right, it's just pointing
out that targeting,

00:12:23.300 --> 00:12:26.100
the targeting of people or invasions

00:12:26.100 --> 00:12:31.023
is a different problem than
the invasion problem itself.

00:12:32.690 --> 00:12:36.920
And so, if all we ever do is think about

00:12:36.920 --> 00:12:39.990
privacy invasion without thinking about

00:12:39.990 --> 00:12:41.440
the discretion problem,

00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:43.440
then we're missing something important.

00:12:43.440 --> 00:12:45.450
- [Kim] For Professor Kerr, however,

00:12:45.450 --> 00:12:48.070
the most important issue at stake today

00:12:48.070 --> 00:12:49.340
regarding the Fourth Amendment

00:12:49.340 --> 00:12:52.310
is how it will apply to electronic media.

00:12:52.310 --> 00:12:53.716
- [Orin] Well, the big question I think is

00:12:53.716 --> 00:12:55.140
how the Fourth Amendment is going to apply

00:12:55.140 --> 00:12:58.270
to searches and seizures
of computers, of emails,

00:12:58.270 --> 00:12:59.532
the Internet,

00:12:59.532 --> 00:13:02.990
new technologies that
we all use every day,

00:13:02.990 --> 00:13:06.090
we wake up in the morning and
hope on line or go on Twitter

00:13:06.090 --> 00:13:09.370
or whatever you use online.

00:13:09.370 --> 00:13:12.270
Lots and lots of communication
online all around the world

00:13:13.140 --> 00:13:15.040
and how does the Fourth
Amendment apply to that

00:13:15.040 --> 00:13:17.470
is something that the courts
are beginning to answer

00:13:17.470 --> 00:13:19.400
but they've only started to answer.

00:13:19.400 --> 00:13:22.210
Some really, really hard
questions of kind of translating

00:13:22.210 --> 00:13:25.300
that physical world concept
of the Fourth Amendment

00:13:25.300 --> 00:13:27.920
to the world of computers
that we live today.

00:13:27.920 --> 00:13:30.650
So as we've learned, the
Fourth Amendment ensures

00:13:30.650 --> 00:13:33.170
that government authorities
can't raid homes,

00:13:33.170 --> 00:13:36.590
pockets or papers without a
warrant issued by a judge,

00:13:36.590 --> 00:13:39.110
and then only if there's a probable cause

00:13:39.110 --> 00:13:41.420
to believe there is reason for a search.

00:13:41.420 --> 00:13:45.040
What's tricky is deciding
what counts as probable cause

00:13:45.040 --> 00:13:47.390
and whether it's discriminatory to select

00:13:47.390 --> 00:13:50.390
some people to search but not others.

00:13:50.390 --> 00:13:52.210
And how do we translate
the Fourth Amendment

00:13:52.210 --> 00:13:55.230
to a world where our
papers are all digital.

00:13:55.230 --> 00:13:57.750
Should the police be permitted
to search our cellphones,

00:13:57.750 --> 00:14:02.100
our browsing histories, our
private social media profiles?

00:14:02.100 --> 00:14:03.990
You can learn more about
the Fourth Amendment

00:14:03.990 --> 00:14:06.520
by checking out the National
Constitution Center's

00:14:06.520 --> 00:14:08.450
interactive constitution

00:14:08.450 --> 00:14:12.423
and Khan Academy's resources
on US government and politics.

